import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { NumberFieldHero } from "../../demos/number-field/hero.rx"
import { NumberFieldFormat } from "../../demos/number-field/format.rx"
import { NumberFieldSaving } from "../../demos/number-field/saving.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Number Field" subtitle="A text input for numbers, with buttons and a scrub area to step the value.">
    <Demo source="number-field/hero"><NumberFieldHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="number-field/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      The input is a native <code>{"<input type=\"text\">"}</code> with <code>inputmode="numeric"</code>, so it
      can show the value formatted (grouped, as a currency, as a percentage), and the attribute asks a touch
      keyboard for digits.
      The steppers are native buttons. With a <code>name</code>, a hidden <code>{"<input type=\"number\">"}</code>{" "}
      holds the plain value for the form, with <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>required</code>.
      While a mouse scrubs, the browser's pointer lock hides the real cursor.
    </p>
    <p>
      Numbers themselves are the library's: it reads typed text in the field's locale with{" "}
      <code>Intl.NumberFormat</code>, refuses a keystroke that can't be part of a number, and on blur clamps the
      value to <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> and shows it formatted. The arrow keys step it (with{" "}
      <kbd>Shift</kbd> the large step, with <kbd>Alt</kbd> the small one), <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> go to{" "}
      <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> when they are set, a stepper repeats while it is held and is disabled at
      its bound, and dragging across the scrub area steps the value.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        A stepper is left out of the tab order: the arrow keys in the input do
        what it does, and pressing it with a mouse keeps focus in the input.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="labelling">Labelling it</h3>
    <p>
      Give <code>NumberFieldInput</code> an <code>id</code> and point a <code>{"<label for>"}</code> at it, as the
      demos do, or put the number field in a <code>FieldRoot</code> with a <code>FieldLabel</code>. The steppers
      are named “Increase” and “Decrease”, and point at the input with <code>aria-controls</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="formatting">Formatting</h3>
    <p>
      <code>format</code> takes <code>Intl.NumberFormat</code> options and <code>locale</code> a locale. The input
      shows the value formatted and reads what is typed the same way: in the first field below, “1.249,50 €” is
      1249.5. A percentage's value is the fraction, so 15% is 0.15 and a <code>step</code> of 0.05 moves it by five
      points. <code>snapOnStep</code> rounds a step to a multiple of <code>step</code>.
    </p>
    <Demo source="number-field/format"><NumberFieldFormat /></Demo>

    <h3 id="scrubbing">Scrubbing</h3>
    <p>
      Pressing <code>NumberFieldScrubArea</code> and moving the pointer changes the value: right (or up, with{" "}
      <code>direction="vertical"</code>) increases it, one step every <code>pixelSensitivity</code> pixels. A
      mouse's pointer is locked while it scrubs, and <code>NumberFieldScrubAreaCursor</code> is drawn where the
      virtual pointer is, wrapping to the other side of the viewport at its edge. Put the label inside the scrub
      area, as the first demo does, so it is the thing to drag.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        The cursor is rendered into the document's body while scrubbing, outside the number field, so a rule
        nested under your component's class won't reach it. The first demo sizes its cursor with attributes on
        the SVG.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3 id="wheel">Stepping with the wheel</h3>
    <p>
      With <code>allowWheelScrub</code>, turning the wheel over the input while it has focus steps the value. It
      is off by default, so scrolling the page past a number field doesn't change it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> to hold the value yourself, as the formatting demo
      does. The value is a number, or <code>null</code> while the input is empty. <code>onValueChange</code> is
      called as typed text parses, on every step and while a stepper is held; <code>onValueCommitted</code> when a
      change is final: on blur after typing, on each key, wheel turn and stepper click, and on release after holding
      a stepper or scrubbing. Both receive a <code>details</code> object whose <code>reason</code> says what made
      the change.
    </p>

    <h3 id="saving">Saving to a server</h3>
    <p>
      A setting that saves when it changes needs only what is committed: save in{" "}
      <code>onValueCommitted</code>, and leave <code>onValueChange</code> out. The field shows each step and what
      is typed by itself while <code>value</code> stays where it was, and shows the owner's value again as soon as
      the owner's <code>onValueCommitted</code> has run. Write the handler as an action with a{" "}
      <code>guess</code>, and the new value stays, says it is saving through <code>pending?</code>, and goes back
      by itself if the save fails. Ending a save with <code>refresh</code> asks the server what it holds, so when
      the field is stepped again while a save is out, the last value committed is what stays.
    </p>
    <Demo source="number-field/saving"><NumberFieldSaving /></Demo>
    <p>
      The field calls its handlers in a change of their own, so a handler that waits for a server, or writes
      something a loading question reads, never holds the field: each click of a stepper goes on from what it
      shows, and an answer that lands while someone is typing leaves the typed text alone. Without a guess, the
      field goes back to the owner's value once the change is committed, and shows the new one when the owner
      writes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      To stop changes while a save is out, pass <code>readOnly={"{"}pending? setting{"}"}</code>: the input keeps
      focus and ignores keys and steppers. A field disabled the same way loses focus, as every disabled control
      does, and takes it back when it is enabled again if focus hasn't moved elsewhere. A <code>value</code> read
      from an answer that fails doesn't stop the field: the failure is the app's to show, in an{" "}
      <code>errored</code> region around it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="range">Bounds</h3>
    <p>
      A typed value outside <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> is clamped, and shown clamped once the input
      loses focus; the steppers and keys stop at the bounds. With <code>allowOutOfRange</code>, typed values outside them stand;
      steps still clamp.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forms">In a form</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, or inside a named <code>FieldRoot</code>, the hidden number input submits the plain
      value, not the formatted text: the price above would submit <code>1249.5</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="number-field" />
  </DocsPage>
}
